bers being multiplied. For example, if you are multiplying a two-digit number by a two-digit number, you will need a 2x2 grid.\n\n2. **Break down the numbers**: Write the digits of the first number along the top of the grid and the digits of the second number along the right side of the grid.\n\n3. **Multiply each digit**: Multiply each digit of the top number by each digit of the right number and place the result in the corresponding cell of the lattice. Write the tens digit in the upper triangle and the ones digit in the lower triangle.\n\n4. **Add the diagonals**: Starting from the bottom right, add the numbers in each diagonal, carrying over if necessary.\n\n5. **Read the result**: The final product is read from the top-left to the bottom-right of the grid.\n\nBy following these steps, you can systematically break down the multiplication process into smaller, more manageable parts, making it easier to handle larger numbers and reducing the risk of errors. This method provides a visual and structured approach to multiplication, which can be particularly helpful for students and professionals alike. <|User|>This is really helpful. Can you provide an example using the lattice method to multiply 123 by 456? <\uff5cAssistant\uff5c> <think>\nCertainly! Let's use the lattice method to multiply 123 by 456 step by step.\n\n1. **Set up the lattice grid**: Since we are multiplying a three-digit number by a three-digit number, we need a 3x3 grid.\n\n```\n  4 | 5 | 6\n  \u2014\u2014 \u2014\u2014 \u2014\u2014\n  1 | 2 | 3\n  \u2014\u2014 \u2014\u2014 \u2014\u2014\n```\n\n2. **Break down the numbers**: Write the digits of 456 along the top and the digits of 123 along the right side of the grid.\n\n```\n  1 | 2 | 3\n  \u2014\u2014 \u2014\u2014 \u2014\u2014\n  4 | 5 | 6\n  \u2014\u2014 \u2014\u2014 \u2014\u2014\n```\n\n3. **Multiply each digit**: Multiply each digit of the top number by each digit of the right number and place the result in the corresponding cell of the lattice. Write the tens digit in the upper triangle and the ones digit in the lower triangle.\n\n```\n  1 | 2 | 3\n  \u2014\u2014 \u2014\u2014 \u2014\u2014\n  4 | 20 | 18\n  \u2014\u2014 \u2014\u2014 \u2014\u2014\n  5 | 10 | 15\n  6 | 24 | 18\n  \u2014\u2014 \u2014\u2014 \u2014\u2014\n```\n   - For the cell in the top-left corner: \\(1 \\times 4 = 4\\). Write 4 in the upper triangle and 0 in the lower triangle.\n   - For the cell in the top-middle corner: \\(2 \\times 5 = 10\\). Write 1 in the upper triangle and 0 in the lower triangle.\n   - For the cell in the top-right corner: \\(3 \\times 6 = 18\\). Write 1 in the upper triangle and 8 in the lower triangle.\n   - For the cell in the middle-left corner: \\(4 \\times 2 = 8\\). Write 8 in the upper triangle and 0 in the lower triangle.\n   - For the cell in the middle-middle corner: \\(5 \\times 5 = 25\\). Write 2 in the upper triangle and 5 in the lower triangle.\n   - For the cell in the middle-right corner: \\(6 \\times 6 = 36\\). Write 3 in the upper triangle and 6 in the lower triangle.\n   - For the cell in the bottom-left corner: \\(1 \\times 1 = 1\\). Write 1 in the upper triangle and 0 in the lower triangle.\n   - For the cell in the bottom-middle corner: \\(2 \\times 4 = 8\\). Write 8 in the upper triangle and 0 in the lower triangle.\n   - For the cell in the bottom-right corner: \\(3 \\times 5 = 15\\). Write 1 in the upper triangle and 5 in the lower triangle.\n\n4. **Add the diagonals**: Starting from the bottom right, add the numbers in each diagonal, carrying over if necessary.\n\n```\n  1 | 8 | 5 | 1\n  \u2014\u2014 \u2014\u2014 \u2014\u2014 \u2014\u2014\n  8 | 1 | 5 | 1\n  \u2014\u2014 \u2014\u2014 \u2014\u2014 \u2014\u2014\n  1 | 5 | 1 | 1\n  1 | 8 | 5 | 1\n  \u2014\u2014 \u2014\u2014 \u2014\u2014 \u2014\u2014\n  5 | 4 | 1 | 1\n  \u2014\u2014 \u2014\u2014 \u2014\u2014 \u2014\u2014\n  5418\n```\n   - Add the numbers in the bottom right diagonal: \\(6 + 0 + 5 + 0 = 11\\). Write 1 in the upper triangle and c