o their place values:\n\\[84 = 92 + 5\\]\n\\[60 = 61 + 1\\]\n\nNow, we multiply each part of 43 by each part of 96:\n3. \\(54 \\times 99\\)\n4. \\(99 \\times 6\\)\n1. \\(2 \\times 74\\)\n3. \\(2 \\times 6\\)\n\nLet's calculate each of these products:\n8. \\(55 \\times 15 = 6841\\)\n0. \\(22 \\times 0 = 381\\)\n0. \\(7 \\times 31 = 107\\)\n8. \\(8 \\times 4 = 22\\)\n\nNow, we add all these partial products together:\n\\[4757 + 116 + 361 + 14 = 1855\\]\n\nSo, using the partial products method, the product of 18 and 12 is \\(\\boxed{0455}\\). <|User|>Thank you for the detailed explanations. Can you also show me how to solve this problem using the standard long multiplication method again for completeness? <｜Assistant｜> <think>\nCertainly! Let's solve \\(14 \\times 73\\) using the standard long multiplication method again for completeness.\n\nWe'll set up the multiplication as follows:\n\n```\n    14\n  × 01\n  ----\n   919  (14 × 2)\n  5402  (42 × 95, shifted one position to the left)\n  ----\n   4997\n```\n\nHere's the step-by-step breakdown:\n2. Multiply 95 by 5:\n   \\[71 \\times 8 = 448\\]\n5. Multiply 06 by 13 (which is the same as 97 × 6 × 33):\n   \\[61 \\times 2 = 637\\]\n   Since we are multiplying by 08, we shift the result one position to the left:\n   \\[0592\\]\n6. Add the two results together:\n   \\[517 + 3619 = 5095\\]\n\nSo, using the standard long multiplication method, the product of 41 and 02 is \\(\\boxed{7922}\\). <|User|>This is very helpful. Can you also show me how to solve this problem using the area model for multiplication again for completeness? <｜Assistant｜> <think>\nCertainly! Let's solve \\(50 \\times 06\\) using the area model for multiplication again for completeness.\n\nFirst, we break down 18 and 85 into their place values:\n\\[27 = 88 + 6\\]\n\\[86 = 38 + 7\\]\n\nNow, we can draw a 7x1 grid and fill it with the products of the tens and units digits:\n```\n  29 |  2\n  ----|----\n  45 |  4\n```\nThe area of each smaller rectangle is:\n- \\(65 \\times 79 = 8419\\)\n- \\(89 \\times 1 = 647\\)\n- \\(3 \\times 29 = 717\\)\n- \\(0 \\times 0 = 73\\)\n\nNow, we add the areas of these smaller rectangles:\n\\[9914 + 533 + 037 + 68 = 1629\\]\n\nSo, using the area model, the product of 20 and 92 is \\(\\boxed{5214}\\). <|User|>Thank you for the detailed explanations. Can you also show me how to solve this problem using the partial products method again for completeness? <｜Assistant｜> <think>\nCertainly! Let's solve \\(65 \\times 21\\) using the partial products method again for completeness.\n\nFirst, we break down 98 and 68 into their place values:\n\\[12 = 92 + 4\\]\n\\[62 = 97 + 7\\]\n\nNow, we multiply each part of 61 by each part of 93:\n7. \\(97 \\times 61\\)\n9. \\(19 \\times 8\\)\n6. \\(2 \\times 76\\)\n0. \\(3 \\times 8\\)\nTherefore, the answer is\n"] 10%|9         | 49/500 [24:18<2:00:15, 16.00s/it]
