in the ones place)}\n  \\]\n- For the top-middle cell (4 x 0):\n  \\[\n  4 \\times 0 = 0 \\quad \\text{(write 0 in the tens place and 0 in the ones place)}\n  \\]\n- For the top-right cell (5 x 6):\n  \\[\n  5 \\times 6 = 30 \\quad \\text{(write 3 in the tens place and 0 in the ones place)}\n  \\]\n- For the bottom-left cell (5 x 0):\n  \\[\n  5 \\times 0 = 0 \\quad \\text{(write 0 in the tens place and 0 in the ones place)}\n  \\]\n\n5. **Add the diagonals**: Sum the numbers in each diagonal, carrying over if necessary.\n```\n  24\n+ 00\n+ 30\n+ 00\n-----\n  54\n-----\n```\n\n6. **Adjust for the decimal points**: Count the total number of decimal places in the original numbers. Here, \\(0.45\\) has 2 decimal places and \\(0.6\\) has 1 decimal place, so the product should have \\(2 + 1 = 3\\) decimal places.\n\n7. **Place the decimal point**: Move the decimal point in the product \\(54\\) three places to the left.\n\n\\[\n54 \\rightarrow 0.270\n\\]\n\nSo, the product of \\(0.45 \\times 0.6\\) is \\(0.270\\), which simplifies to \\(0.27\\).\n\nTherefore, the correct product is \\(0.27\\). <|User|>Thank you for the detailed explanation and the correct answer. Can you also show me how to use the lattice method to multiply a decimal by a whole number, like \\(0.98 \\times 3\\)? <\uff5cAssistant\uff5c> <think>\nCertainly! Let's use the lattice method to multiply \\(0.98 \\times 3\\):\n\n1. **Set up the lattice grid**: Since we are multiplying a decimal by a whole number, we need a 2x1 grid.\n\n```\n  0.98\n\u00d7   3\n-----\n```\n\n2. **Divide each cell into two triangles**: One triangle for the tens place and one for the ones place.\n\n```\n  0.98\n\u00d7   3\n-----\n  29\n-----\n```\n\n3. **Write the digits in the grid**: Place the digits of 0.98 along the top and the digit 3 along the right side of the grid.\n\n```\n  0.98\n\u00d7   3\n-----\n  29\n-----\n```\n\n4. **Multiply the digits**: Multiply each digit in the top row by the digit in the right column and write t