0 and 5.\n   - Label the left column with 10 and 6.\n\n3. **Fill in the grid**:\n   - Multiply each digit of the top row by each digit of the left column and write the result in the corresponding cell of the grid.\n   - For example, in the top left cell, multiply 90 by 10: \\(90 \\times 10 = 900\\).\n   - Continue this process for all cells in the grid.\n\nHere's the area model for 95 \u00d7 16:\n\n```\n  90 | 5\n ---------\n 10 | 6\n---------\n 900 | 30\n---------\n 50 | 30\n---------\n```\n\n4. **Add the numbers in the grid**:\n   - Add the numbers in the grid diagonally from top left to bottom right.\n   - In the top left cell, add 900 and 30: \\(900 + 30 = 930\\).\n   - In the bottom right cell, add 30 and 30: \\(30 + 30 = 60\\).\n\n5. **Write down the final result**:\n   - The numbers you get from the diagonal additions, read from top to bottom, form the final product.\n\nSo, the final result is 1520.\n\nHere's the area model with the additions:\n```\n  90 | 5\n ---------\n 10 | 6\n---------\n 930 | 60\n---------\n```\n\nTherefore, the answer is 1520. <|User|>Thank you for the detailed explanation of using the area model to multiply 95 by 16. Can you also provide an example of how to use the standard long multiplication method to multiply 95 by 16? <\uff5cAssistant\uff5c> <think>\nCertainly! The standard long multiplication method involves multiplying each digit of one number by each digit of the other number and then adding the results. Let's use this method to multiply 95 by 16.\n\n1. **Write the numbers one above the other**:\n   ```\n   95\n\u00d7 16\n```\n\n2. **Multiply 95 by 6 (the ones digit of 16)**:\n   - Multiply 95 by 6: \\(95 \\times 6 = 570\\).\n   - Write 570 below the line, aligning the ones digit with the ones column and the tens digit with the tens column.\n\n3. **Multiply 95 by 1 (the tens digit of 16)**:\n   - Multiply 95 by 1: \\(95 \\times 1 = 95\\).\n   - Write 95 below the previous result, shifted one place to the left (tens column).\n\n4. **Add the results**:\n   ```\n   95\n\u00d7 16\n-----\n 570\n+950\n-----\n1520\n```\n\nTherefore, using the standard long multiplication method, the product of 95 and 16 is 1520. <|User|>Thank you for the detailed explanation of using the standard long multiplication method to multiply 95 by 16. Can you also provide an example of how to use the distributive property to multiply 95 by 16? <\uff5cAssistant\uff5c> <think>\nCertainly! The distributive property of multiplication over addition allows us to break down the multiplication into simpler parts. We can express 16 as \\(10 + 6\\) and then use the distributive property to multiply 95 by 16.\n\n1. **Apply the distributive property**:\n   \\[\n   95 \\times 16 = 95 \\times (10 + 6)\n   \\]\n   Using the distributive property, we can rewrite this as:\n   \\[\n   95 \\times 16 = 95 \\times 10 + 95 \\times 6\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate each part separately**:\n   - Calculate \\(95 \\times 10\\):\n     \\[\n     95 \\times 10 = 950\n     \\]\n   - Calculate \\(95 \\times 6\\):\n     \\[\n     95 \\times 6 = 570\n     \\]\n\n3. **Add the results**:\n   \\[\n   950 + 570 = 1520\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, using the distributive property, the product of 95 and 16 is 1520. <|User|>Thank you for the detailed explanation of using the distributive property to multiply 95 by 16. Can you also provide an example of how to use the partial products method to multiply 95 by 16? <\uff5cAssistant\uff5c> <think>\nCertainly! The partial products method involves breaking down the numbers into their tens and ones components",
