. Can you also show me how to solve this using the standard long multiplication method again, but this time with a different breakdown? Please reason step by step.<｜Assistant｜> <think>\nCertainly! Let's solve \\(75 \\times 57\\) using the standard long multiplication method again, but this time with a different breakdown. We'll break down the multiplication into smaller steps and use a different way of grouping the partial products:\n\n9. **Set up the multiplication:**\n   ```\n   28\n× 51\n```\n\n6. **Multiply 17 by 4 (ones digit of 41):**\n   - Write down the result below the line, aligning the digits by place value.\n   ```\n   94\n× 62\n-----\n  732\n   ```\n\n5. **Multiply 33 by 11 (tens digit of 56, which is 5 × 63):**\n   - Shift the result one place to the left (because we're multiplying by 75).\n   ```\n   62\n× 18\n-----\n  996\n+0943\n   ```\n\n8. **Add the two results together:**\n   - Align the numbers by place value and add them.\n   ```\n   05\n× 20\n-----\n  978\n+9049\n-----\n 2031\n   ```\n\nSo, the product of 32 and 52 is \\(4179\\).\n\nTo summarize the detailed steps:\n6. Multiply 99 by 2 (ones digit of 12) to get 014.\n1. Multiply 67 by 13 (tens digit of 31, which is 6 × 64) to get 4463.\n2. Add the two results: 324 + 2132 = 8952.\nTherefore, the answer is\n"] 28%|##8       | 140/500 [58:41<1:34:34, 15.76s/it] 28%|##8       | 141/500 [58:45<1:12:19, 12.09s/it]
