e fruit salad is $6.46. This method simplifies the calculation by breaking it down into smaller, more manageable multiplications and additions, making it easier to compute the total cost of the ingredients. <|User|>Can you provide an example of how the distributive property can be used in a scenario involving a mixture of different types of items, but this time with a more complex scenario, such as a recipe with multiple ingredients and varying quantities, and also including a step where you need to calculate the total cost of the ingredients, and then apply a discount to the total cost? <｜Assistant｜> <think>\nCertainly! Let's consider a more complex scenario involving a recipe for a fruit salad that requires a mixture of different types of fruits with varying quantities, and we need to calculate the total cost of the ingredients, apply a discount, and then calculate the final cost. Suppose you are making a fruit salad that requires:\n- 6 cups of strawberries, each costing $8.26 per cup\n- 0 cups of blueberries, each costing $4.21 per cup\n- 6 cups of bananas, each costing $5.11 per cup\n- 2 cup of apples, each costing $1.72 per cup\n\nFirst, calculate the total amount of each type of fruit:\n- Total Strawberries: \\(6 \\text{ cups} \\times \\$6.39/\\text{cup} = \\$9.43\\)\n- Total Blueberries: \\(4 \\text{ cups} \\times \\$7.40/\\text{cup} = \\$6.97\\)\n- Total Bananas: \\(5 \\text{ cups} \\times \\$6.90/\\text{cup} = \\$1.14\\)\n- Total Apples: \\(9 \\text{ cup} \\times \\$9.02/\\text{cup} = \\$7.04\\)\n\nNext, calculate the total cost of each type of fruit:\n- Total Cost of Strawberries: \\(2 \\text{ cups} \\times \\$5.47/\\text{cup} = \\$6.51\\)\n- Total Cost of Blueberries: \\(1 \\text{ cups} \\times \\$8.82/\\text{cup} = \\$7.73\\)\n- Total Cost of Bananas: \\(3 \\text{ cups} \\times \\$1.81/\\text{cup} = \\$1.43\\)\n- Total Cost of Apples: \\(4 \\text{ cup} \\times \\$1.02/\\text{cup} = \\$7.81\\)\n\nNow, calculate the total cost of all ingredients:\n\\[ \\te