ep:\n1. Calculate the total number of gadgets sold:\n   \\[ a + b = 30 + 45 = 75 \\]\n2. Calculate the total price per gadget:\n   \\[ p_a + p_b = 7 + 5 = 12 \\]\n3. Multiply the total number of gadgets by the total price per gadget:\n   \\[ R = 75 \\cdot 12 \\]\n\nTo make the multiplication easier, we can break it down:\n\\[ 75 \\cdot 12 = 75 \\cdot (10 + 2) = (75 \\cdot 10) + (75 \\cdot 2) \\]\n\nNow, let's calculate each part separately:\n1. Calculate \\( 75 \\cdot 10 \\):\n   \\[ 75 \\cdot 10 = 750 \\]\n2. Calculate \\( 75 \\cdot 2 \\):\n   \\[ 75 \\cdot 2 = 150 \\]\n\nFinally, add the two results together:\n\\[ R = 750 + 150 = 900 \\]\n\nSo, the total revenue from selling 30 gadgets of Type X and 45 gadgets of Type Y is $900. This method simplifies the calculation by breaking it down into two simpler multiplications, making it easier to manage and understand.\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>Certainly! Let's consider another example to further illustrate the distributive property. Suppose a company sells two types of gadgets: Type X and Type Y. The company sells 30 gadgets of Type X at $7 each and 45 gadgets of Type Y at $5 each. To find the total revenue from selling these gadgets, we can use the distributive property.\n\nLet's denote:\n- The number of gadgets of Type X as \\( a = 30 \\)\n- The price per gadget of Type X as \\( p_a = 7 \\)\n- The number of gadgets of Type Y as \\( b = 45 \\)\n- The price per gadget of Type Y as \\( p_b = 5 \\)\n\nThe total revenue \\( R \\) can be calculated as:\n\\[ R = a \\cdot p_a + b \\cdot p_b \\]\n\nUsing the distributive property, we can rewrite this as:\n\\[ R = (a + b) \\cdot (p_a + p_b) \\]\n\nNow, let's calculate it step-by-step:\n1. Calculate the total number of gadgets sold:\n   \\[ a + b = 30 + 45 = 75 \\]\n2. Calculate the total price per gadget:\n   \\[ p_a + p_b = 7 + 5 = 12 \\]\n3. Multiply the total number of gadgets by the total price per gadget:\n   \\[ R = 75 \\cdot 12 \\]\n\nTo make the multiplication easier, we 