d to visualize the multiplication:\n```\n  80 + 3\n\u00d7  90 + 6\n---------\n  7200 + 480  (80 \u00d7 90 and 3 \u00d7 90)\n  7200 + 180  (80 \u00d7 6 and 3 \u00d7 6)\n---------\n  8088\n```\nBy adding the products in the grid, we get:\n- 80 \u00d7 90 = 7200\n- 80 \u00d7 6 = 480\n- 3 \u00d7 90 = 270\n- 3 \u00d7 6 = 18\nAdding these together: 7200 + 480 + 270 + 18 = 8068.\n\nSo, using the area model, we also get the result: 83 \u00d7 96 = 8068. <|User|>Thank you for the detailed explanation using the area model. It's very helpful.\n<|Assistant|>You're welcome! If you have any more questions or need further explanations, feel free to ask. Understanding these different methods will help you tackle multiplication problems more effectively. <|User|>Can you also show me how to solve this problem using the partial products method?\n<|Assistant|>Certainly! The partial products method is another way to break down multiplication into simpler parts. For the problem 83 \u00d7 96, we can use the partial products method by breaking down each number into its tens and ones place values and then multiplying each part.\n\nLet's break down 83 and 96:\n- 83 = 80 + 3\n- 96 = 90 + 6\n\nNow, we'll multiply each part of 83 by each part of 96 and then add the results:\n1. 80 \u00d7 90 = 7200\n2. 80 \u00d7 6 = 480\n3. 3 \u00d7 90 = 270\n4. 3 \u00d7 6 = 18\n\nNow, we add these partial products together:\n7200 + 480 + 270 + 18 = 8068\n\nSo, using the partial products method, we get the same result: 83 \u00d7 96 = 8068.\n\nHere's a step-by-step breakdown:\n```\n  80 \u00d7 90 = 7200\n  80 \u00d7 6 = 480\n  3 \u00d7 90 = 270\n  3 \u00d7 6 = 18\n---------\n  8068\n```\n\nThis method helps to break down the multiplication into smaller, more manageable steps, making it easier to understand and compute. <|User|>Thank you for the detailed explanation using the partial products method. It's very clear now.\n<|Assistant|>You're welcome! If you have any more questions or need further explanations, feel 