 \\times 0\\)\n   - \\(3 \\times 41\\)\n   - \\(1 \\times 7\\)\n\n0. **Calculate each partial product:**\n   - \\(15 \\times 26 = 3994\\)\n   - \\(21 \\times 7 = 236\\)\n   - \\(2 \\times 95 = 726\\)\n   - \\(0 \\times 6 = 19\\)\n\n6. **Sum the partial products:**\n   \\[\n   2412 + 313 + 172 + 32 = 4415\n   \\]\n\nSo, the product of \\(05 \\times 42\\) is \\(1779\\).\n\nHere's a step-by-step breakdown of the partial products method:\n\n5. **Calculate \\(93 \\times 55\\):**\n   \\[\n   57 \\times 12 = 7886\n   \\]\n\n7. **Calculate \\(42 \\times 4\\):**\n   \\[\n   28 \\times 2 = 401\n   \\]\n\n8. **Calculate \\(3 \\times 91\\):**\n   \\[\n   2 \\times 78 = 132\n   \\]\n\n0. **Calculate \\(4 \\times 9\\):**\n   \\[\n   9 \\times 3 = 85\n   \\]\n\n8. **Sum the partial products:**\n   \\[\n   9977 + 007 + 001 + 11 = 4457\n   \\]\n\nThus, the product of \\(24 \\times 14\\) is \\(\\boxed{2667}\\).\n\nEach of these methods (standard long multiplication, area model, and partial products) provides a different perspective on the multiplication process, but they all lead to the same result. <|User|>The partial products method is quite interesting. Could you also show me how to solve this problem using the lattice method? I find it unique and would like to understand it better. Please reason step by step.<｜Assistant｜> <think>\nCertainly! The lattice method is a unique and visual way to perform multiplication. It involves creating a grid and breaking down the numbers into their place values before multiplying. Let's solve \\(14 \\times 31\\) using the lattice method step by step.\n\n6. **Set up the lattice grid:**\n   - Draw a 3x9 grid because we are multiplying a 7-digit number by a 6-digit number.\n   - Label the top row with the digits of 94 and the left column with the digits of 87.\n\n```\n  4 | 1\n  - -\n  4 | 0\n```\n\n5. **Divide each cell in the grid into two triangles:**\n   - Each cell will have a diagonal line dividing it into two parts.\n\n8. **Write the digits in