erty.\n\n1. **Original Area:**\n   - The original dimensions of the garden are 10 meters by 5 meters.\n   - The original area is \\( 10 \\times 5 = 50 \\) square meters.\n\n2. **Expanding the Garden:**\n   - Adding a 2-meter wide border around the garden increases the length and width by 4 meters (2 meters on each side).\n   - The new dimensions are \\( (10 + 4) \\) meters by \\( (5 + 4) \\) meters, which simplifies to 14 meters by 9 meters.\n   - The new area is \\( 14 \\times 9 \\).\n\n3. **Using the Distributive Property:**\n   - Instead of calculating the new area directly, you can use the distributive property to break it down:\n   - \\( 14 \\times 9 = (10 + 4) \\times 9 \\)\n   - Apply the distributive property: \\( 10 \\times 9 + 4 \\times 9 \\)\n   - Calculate each part separately:\n     - \\( 10 \\times 9 = 90 \\)\n     - \\( 4 \\times 9 = 36 \\)\n   - Add the results: \\( 90 + 36 = 126 \\)\n\nTherefore, the new area of the garden including the border is 126 square meters.\n\nThis example shows how the distributive property can be used to simplify area calculations in real-life scenarios.\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>Certainly! Let's consider a scenario involving area calculations. Suppose you have a rectangular garden that is 10 meters long and 5 meters wide, but you want to expand it by adding a 2-meter wide border around it. To find the new area of the garden including the border, you can use the distributive property.\n\n1. **Original Area:**\n   - The original dimensions of the garden are 10 meters by 5 meters.\n   - The original area is \\( 10 \\times 5 = 50 \\) square meters.\n\n2. **Expanding the Garden:**\n   - Adding a 2-meter wide border around the garden increases the length and width by 4 meters (2 meters on each side).\n   - The new dimensions are \\( (10 + 4) \\) meters by \\( (5 + 4) \\) meters, which simplifies to 14 meters by 9 meters.\n   - The new area is \\( 14 \\times 9 \\).\n\n3. **Using the Distributive Property:**\n   - Instead of calcul