36\n   ```\n\nTherefore, the product of 94 and 44 is 4136. <|User|>Okay, I understand the process now. Can you show me how to solve 94 multiplied by 44 using the distributive property?\n<think>\nCertainly! The distributive property allows us to break down the multiplication into simpler parts. We can express 94 as (90 + 4) and 44 as (40 + 4). Then, we can use the distributive property to expand the multiplication:\n\n94 \u00d7 44 = (90 + 4) \u00d7 (40 + 4)\n\nWe can distribute each term in the first parenthesis to each term in the second parenthesis:\n\n= 90 \u00d7 40 + 90 \u00d7 4 + 4 \u00d7 40 + 4 \u00d7 4\n\nNow, let's calculate each of these products:\n\n90 \u00d7 40 = 3600\n90 \u00d7 4 = 360\n4 \u00d7 40 = 160\n4 \u00d7 4 = 16\n\nNow, we add these results together:\n\n3600 + 360 + 160 + 16 = 4136\n\nSo, 94 \u00d7 44 = 4136.\n...\n</think>\n<answer>\nTo solve 94 multiplied by 44 using the distributive property, we can break down the numbers into simpler components and then use the distributive property to expand the multiplication. Here's how we can do it step by step:\n\n1. Express 94 as (90 + 4) and 44 as (40 + 4):\n   94 \u00d7 44 = (90 + 4) \u00d7 (40 + 4)\n\n2. Apply the distributive property:\n   (90 + 4) \u00d7 (40 + 4) = 90 \u00d7 40 + 90 \u00d7 4 + 4 \u00d7 40 + 4 \u00d7 4\n\n3. Calculate each of these products:\n   90 \u00d7 40 = 3600\n   90 \u00d7 4 = 360\n   4 \u00d7 40 = 160\n   4 \u00d7 4 = 16\n\n4. Add these results together:\n   3600 + 360 + 160 + 16 = 4136\n\nTherefore, 94 \u00d7 44 = 4136. <|User|>That makes sense. Can you show me how to solve 94 multiplied by 44 using the area model?\n<think>\nCertainly! The area model is a visual method that helps us break down the multiplication into smaller, more manageable parts. We can represent 94 and 44 as lengths of sides of a rectangle, and the product will be the area of that rectangle. Here's how we can use the area model to solve 94 \u00d7 44:\n\n1. Break down 94 into 90 and 4.\n2. Break down 44 into 40 and 4.\n3. Draw a rectangle and divide it into four smaller rectangles based on these breakdowns.\n4. Calculate the area of each smaller rectangle:\n   - The area of the rectangle with sides 90 and 40 is 90 \u00d7 40 = 3600.\n   - The area of the rectangle with sides 90 and 4 is 90 \u00d7 4 = 360.\n   - The area of the rectangle with sides 40 and 4 is 40 \u00d7 4 = 160.\n   - The area of the rectangle with sides 4 and 4 is 4 \u00d7 4 = 16.\n5. Add the areas of these smaller rectangles together to get the total area:\n   3600 + 360 + 160 + 16 = 4136\n\nSo, 94 \u00d7 44 = 4136.\n...\n</think>\n<answer>\nTo solve 94 multiplied by 44 using the area model, we can visualize the multiplication as the area of a rectangle. Here's how we can break it down step by step:\n\n1. Break down 94 into 90 and 4.\n2. Break down 44 into 40 and 4.\n3. Draw a large rectangle and divide it into four smaller rectangles:\n   - One rectangle with sides 90 and 40.\n   - One rectangle with sides 90 and 4.\n   - One rectangle with sides 40 and 4.\n   - One rectangle with sides 4 and 4.\n4. Calculate the area of each smaller rectangle:\n   - The area of the rectangle with sides 90 and 40 is 90 \u00d7 40 = 3600.\n   - The area of the rectangle with sides 90 and 4 is 90 \u00d7 4 = 360.\n   - The area of the rectangle with sides 40 and 4 is 40 \u00d7 4 = 160.\n   - The area of the rectangle with sides 4 and 4 is 4 \u00d7 4 = 16.\n5. Add the areas of these smaller rectangles together to get the total area:\n   3600 + 360 + 160 + 16 = 4136\n\nTherefore, 94 \u00d7 44 = 4136. <|User|>Can you explain how to solve 94 multiplied by 44 using the partial products method?\n<think>\nCertainly! The partial products method involves breaking down each number into its place values and then multiplying each part separately before adding the results together. Here's how we can use the partial products method to solve 94 \u00d7 44:\n\n1. Break down 94 into 90 and 4.\n2. Break down 44 into 40 and 4.\n3. Multiply each part of 94 by each part of 44:\n   - 90 \u00d7 40 = 3600\n   - 90 \u00d7 4 = 360\n   - 4 \u00d7 40 = 160\n   - 4 \u00d7 4 = 16\n4. Add these partial products together:\n   3600 + 360 + 160 + 16 = 4136\n\nSo, 94 \u00d7 44 = 4136.\n...\n</think>\n<answer>\nTo solve 94 multiplied by 44 using the partial products method, we break down each number into its place values and then multiply each part separately before adding the results together. Here's how we can do it step by step:\n\n1. Break down 94 into 90 and 4.\n2. Break down 44 into 40 and 4.\n3. Multiply each part of 94 by each part of 44:\n   - 90 \u00d7 40 = 3600\n   - 90 \u00d7 4 = 360\n   - 4 \u00d7 40 = 160\n   - 4 \u00d7 4 = 16\n4. Add these partial products together:\n   3600 + 360 + 160 + 16 = 4136\n\nTherefore, 94 \u00d7 44 = 4136. <|User|>Can you explain how to solve 94 multiplied by 44 using the standard algorithm?\n<think>\nCertainly! The standard algorithm for multiplication involves multiplying each digit of one number by each digit of the other number, starting from the rightmost digits, and then adding the results together. Here's how we can use the standard algorithm to solve 94 \u00d7 44:\n\n1. Write the numbers one above the other:\n   ```\n   94\n   \u00d744\n   ```\n\n2. Multiply 4 (from 44) by each digit of 94, starting from the rightmost digit:\n   - 4 \u00d7 4 = 16 (write down 6, carry over 1)\n   - 4 \u00d7 9 = 36 (add the carried over 1, which makes it 37)\n\n3. Write down the results, aligning them by place value:\n   ```\n   94\n   \u00d744\n   -----\n    376\n   ```\n\n4. Now, multiply 40 (from 44) by each digit of 94, starting from the rightmost digit:\n   - 40 \u00d7 4 = 160 (write down 60, carry over 1)\n   - 40 \u00d7 9 = 360 (add the carried over 1, which makes it 361)\n\n5. Write down the results, aligning them by place value:\n   ```\n   94\n   \u00d744\n   -----\n    376\n   +3610\n   -----\n   4136\n   ```\n\n6. Add the two results together:\n   ```\n   94\n   \u00d744\n   -----\n    376\n   +3610\n   -----\n   4136\n   ```\n\nTherefore, 94 \u00d7 44 = 4136.\n...\n</think>\n<answer>\nTo solve 94 multiplied by 44 using the standard algorithm, we follow these steps:\n\n1. Write the numbers one above the other:\n   ```\n   94\n   \u00d744\n   ```\n\n2. Multiply 4 (from 44) by each digit of 94, starting from the rightmost digit:\n   - 4 \u00d7 4 = 16 (write down 6, carry over 1)\n   - 4 \u00d7 9 = 36 (add the carried over 1, which makes it 37)\n\n3. Write down the results, aligning them by place value:\n   ```\n   94\n   \u00d744\n   -----\n    376\n   ```\n\n4. Now, multiply 40 (from 44) by each digit of 94, starting from the rightmost digit:\n   - 40 \u00d7 4 = 160 (write down 60, carry over 1)\n   - 40 \u00d7 9 = 360 (add the carried over 1, which makes it 361)\n\n5. Write down the results, aligning them by place value:\n   ```\n   94\n   \u00d744\n   -----\n    376\n   +3610\n   -----\n   4136\n   ```\n\n6. Add the two results together:\n   ```\n   94\n   \u00d744\n   -----\n    376\n   +3610\n   -----\n   4136\n   ```\n\nTherefore, 94 \u00d7 44 = 4136. <|User|>Can you explain how to solve 94 multiplied by 44 using the lattice method?\n<think>\nCertainly! The lattice method is a visual way to multiply two numbers by breaking them down into smaller parts and then summing up the results. Here's how we can use the lattice method to solve 94 \u00d7 44:\n\n1. Draw a 2x2 grid (since 94 and 44 are both 2-digit numbers).\n2. Write the digits of 94 along the top of the grid and the digits of 44 along the right side of the grid.\n3. Multiply each digit of 94 by each digit of 44, placing the tens digit in the upper triangle and the ones digit in the lower triangle of each cell.\n4. Add the numbers in each diagonal, carrying over if necessary.\n5. Read the result from the left and bottom edges of the grid.\n\nLet's apply these steps to 94 \u00d7 44:\n\n1. Draw a 2x2 grid:\n   ```\n   94\n   \u00d744\n   ----\n   ```\n\n2. Write the digits of 94 along the top and 44 along the right side:\n   ```\n   94\n   \u00d744\n   ----\n   ```\n\n3. Multiply each digit:\n   ```\n   94\n   \u00d744\n   ----\n   3600  (9 \u00d7 4)\n   376   (9 \u00d7 40)\n   360   (4 \u00d7 4)\n   16    (4 \u00d7 40)\n   ----\n   ```\n\n4. Add the numbers in each diagonal, carrying over if necessary:\n   ```\n   94\n   \u00d744\n   ----\n   3600  (9 \u00d7 4)\n   376   (9 \u00d7 40)\n   360   (4 \u00d7 4)\n   16    (4 \u00d7 40)\n   ----\n   4136\n   ```\n\n5. Read the result from the left and bottom edges of the grid:\n   ```\n   94\n   \u00d744\n   ----\n   4136\n   ```\n\nTherefore, 94 \u00d7 44 = 4136.\n...\n</think>\n<answer>\nTo solve 94 multiplied by 44 using the lattice method, we follow these steps:\n\n1. Draw a 2x2 grid (since 94 and 44 are both 2-digit numbers):\n   ```\n   94\n   \u00d744\n   ----\n   ```\n\n2. Write the digits of 94 along the top and 44 along the right side:\n   ```\n   94\n   \u00d744\n   ----\n   ```\n\n3. Multiply each digit:\n   ```\n   94\n   \u00d744\n   ----\n   3600  (9 \u00d7 4)\n   376   (9 \u00d7 40)\n   360   (4 \u00d7 4)\n   16    (4 \u00d7 40)\n   ----\n   ```\n\n4. Add the numbers in each diagonal, carrying over if necessary:\n   ```\n   94\n   \u00d744\n   ----\n   3600  (9 \u00d7 4)\n   376   (9 \u00d7 40)\n   360   (4 \u00d7 4)\n   16    (4 \u00d7 40)\n   ----\n   4136\n   ```\n\n5. Read the result from the left and bottom edges of the grid:\n   ```\n   94\n   \u00d744\n   ----\n   4136\n   ```\n\nTherefore, 94 \u00d7 44 = 4136. <|User|>Can you explain how to solve 94 multiplied by 44 using the grid method?\n<think>\nCertainly! The grid method, also known as the box method, is a visual way to multiply two numbers by breaking them down into smaller parts and then summing up the results. Here's how we can use the grid method to solve 94 \u00d7 44:\n\n1. Draw a 2x2 grid (since 94 and 44 are both 2-digit numbers).\n2. Write the digits of 94 along the top of the grid and the digits of 44 along t