      "text": "3639. <\uff5cUser\uff5c>It seems there was a mistake in the initial multiplication breakdown. Let's correct it step by step:\n\n1. We need to find the product of 14 and 73.\n2. We can use the standard multiplication method:\n   - First, multiply 14 by 3 (the units digit of 73):\n     14 \u00d7 3 = 42\n   - Next, multiply 14 by 70 (the tens digit of 73, which is 7 \u00d7 10):\n     14 \u00d7 70 = 980\n3. Now, we add the two results together:\n   42 + 980 = 1022\n\nTherefore, the correct product of 14 and 73 is 1022.\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>You are correct. The product of 14 and 73 is 1022. Thank you for catching the mistake in my initial response. The correct calculation is as follows:\n\n1. Multiply 14 by 3 (the units digit of 73):\n   14 \u00d7 3 = 42\n2. Multiply 14 by 70 (the tens digit of 73, which is 7 \u00d7 10):\n   14 \u00d7 70 = 980\n3. Add the two results together:\n   42 + 980 = 1022\n\nTherefore, the product of 14 and 73 is 1022. <\uff5cUser\uff5c>Thank you for the detailed explanation and correction. It's clear now.\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>You're welcome! If you have any more questions or need further clarification, feel free to ask. I'm here to help. <\uff5cUser\uff5c>You're very helpful. Can you also show me how to solve this problem using the distributive property?\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>Certainly! The distributive property allows us to break down the multiplication into simpler parts. For the problem 14 \u00d7 73, we can use the distributive property as follows:\n\n1. Break down 73 into 70 + 3:\n   14 \u00d7 73 = 14 \u00d7 (70 + 3)\n2. Apply the distributive property, which states that a(b + c) = ab + ac:\n   14 \u00d7 (70 + 3) = (14 \u00d7 70) + (14 \u00d7 3)\n3. Calculate each part separately:\n   - 14 \u00d7 70 = 980\n   - 14 \u00d7 3 = 42\n4. Add the results together:\n   980 + 42 = 1022\n\nSo, using the distributive property, we get the same result: 14 \u00d7 73 = 1022. <\uff5cUser\uff5c>That makes sense. Thank you for walking me through it.\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>You're welcome! If you have any more questions or need further explanations, feel free to ask. Understanding the distributive property can be very helpful in solving multiplication problems. <\uff5cUser\uff5c>Can you give me an example of how to use the distributive property in a different multiplication problem?\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>Of course! Let's use the distributive property to solve a different multiplication problem, say 12 \u00d7 17. We can break down 17 into 10 + 7:\n\n1. Apply the distributive property:\n   12 \u00d7 17 = 12 \u00d7 (10 + 7)\n2. Distribute 12 to both 10 and 7:\n   12 \u00d7 (10 + 7) = (12 \u00d7 10) + (12 \u00d7 7)\n3. Calculate each part separately:\n   - 12 \u00d7 10 = 120\n   - 12 \u00d7 7 = 84\n4. Add the results together:\n   120 + 84 = 204\n\nSo, using the distributive property, we get:\n12 \u00d7 17 = 204\n\nThis method can be applied to any multiplication problem where one of the factors can be easily broken down into simpler parts. <\uff5cUser\uff5c>Thank you for the example. It's really helpful to see how the distributive property works in different scenarios.\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>You're welcome! If you have any more questions or need further examples, feel free to ask. The distributive property is a powerful tool in mathematics that can simplify many calculations. <\uff5cUser\uff5c>Can you provide another example using the distributive property for a more complex number, like 15 \u00d7 37?\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>Certainly! Let's use the distributive property to solve 15 \u00d7 37. We can break down 37 into 30 + 7:\n\n1. Apply the distributive property:\n   15 \u00d7 37 = 15 \u00d7 (30 + 7)\n2. Distribute 15 to both 30 and 7:\n   15 \u00d7 (30 + 7) = (15 \u00d7 30) + (15 \u00d7 7)\n3. Calculate each part separately:\n   - 15 \u00d7 30 = 450\n   - 15 \u00d7 7 = 105\n4. Add the results together:\n   450 + 105 = 555\n\nSo, using the distributive property, we get:\n15 \u00d7 37 = 555\n\nThis method can be applied to any multiplication problem where one of the factors can be easily broken down into simpler parts. <\uff5cUser\uff5c>Thank you for the detailed example. It's clear now how to use the distributive property for more complex numbers.\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>You're welcome! If you have any more questions or need further examples, feel free to ask. Using the distributive property can simplify calculations and make them easier to manage, especially with larger numbers. <\uff5cUser\uff5c>Can you provide an example of how to use the distributive property in a real-world scenario, like calculating the total cost of items in a shopping cart?\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>Certainly! Let's consider a real-world scenario where you're shopping and need to calculate the total cost of items in a shopping cart. Suppose you have the following items:\n\n- 3 shirts, each costing $20\n- 2 pairs of pants, each costing $40\n- 1 bag, costing $30\n\nWe can use the distributive property to calculate the total cost. Let's break it down:\n\n1. Calculate the cost of the shirts:\n   - 3 shirts \u00d7 $20 per shirt = (3 \u00d7 20) = 60\n2. Calculate the cost of the pants:\n   - 2 pairs of pants \u00d7 $40 per pair = (2 \u00d7 40) = 80\n3. Calculate the cost of the bag:\n   - 1 bag \u00d7 $30 = 30\n\nNow, we can use the distributive property to add these amounts together:\n\n1. Add the costs of the shirts and pants:\n   - 60 (shirts) + 80 (pants) = 140\n2. Add the cost of the bag to the result:\n   - 140 (shirts and pants) + 30 (bag) = 170\n\nSo, the total cost of the items in the shopping cart is $170.\n\nThis method can be applied to any real-world scenario where you need to calculate the total cost of multiple items with different quantities and prices. <\uff5cUser\uff5c>That's a great example. It's clear how the distributive property can be used in practical situations.\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>You're welcome! If you have any more questions or need further examples, feel free to ask. Using the distributive property in real-world scenarios can help simplify calculations and make them more manageable. <\uff5cUser\uff5c>Can you provide another real-world example using the distributive property, perhaps involving a budget for a project?\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>Certainly! Let's consider a budgeting scenario for a project where you need to allocate funds for different components. Suppose you are planning a project that requires the following expenses:\n\n- Labor costs: $10,000\n- Materials costs: $5,000\n- Equipment costs: $3,000\n\nWe can use the distributive property to calculate the total budget for the project. Let's break it down:\n\n1. Calculate the total budget:\n   - Total budget = Labor costs + Materials costs + Equipment costs\n   - Total budget = (10,000 + 5,000 + 3,000)\n\nNow, we can use the distributive property to add these amounts together:\n\n1. Add the labor and materials costs:\n   - 10,000 (labor) + 5,000 (materials) = 15,000\n2. Add the equipment costs to the result:\n   - 15,000 (labor and materials) + 3,000 (equipment) = 18,000\n\nSo, the total budget for the project is $18,000.\n\nThis method can be applied to any budgeting scenario where you need to allocate funds for multiple components. <\uff5cUser\uff5c>That's a great example. It's clear how the distributive property can be used in budgeting scenarios.\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>You're welcome! If you have any more questions or need further examples, feel free to ask. Using the distributive property in budgeting scenarios can help simplify calculations and make them more manageable. <\uff5cUser\uff5c>Can you provide an example of how to use the distributive property in a financial planning context, such as calculating the total interest on a loan?\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>Certainly! Let's consider a financial planning scenario where you need to calculate the total interest on a loan. Suppose you have a loan with the following details:\n\n- Principal amount (P): $10,000\n- Annual interest rate (r): 5% (or 0.05 as a decimal)\n- Number of years (t): 3\n\nWe can use the formula for simple interest, which is:\n\n\\[ \\text{Simple Interest} = P \\times r \\times t \\]\n\nHowever, we can also use the distributive property to simplify the calculation. Let's break it down:\n\n1. Calculate the interest for each year:\n   - Interest for the first year = $10,000 \u00d7 0.05 \u00d7 1 = $500\n   - Interest for the second year = $10,000 \u00d7 0.05 \u00d7 1 = $500\n   - Interest for the third year = $10,000 \u00d7 0.05 \u00d7 1 = $500\n\nNow, we can use the distributive property to add these amounts together:\n\n1. Add the interest for each year:\n   - $500 (first year) + $500 (second year) + $500 (third year) = $1,500\n\nSo, the total interest on the loan over 3 years is $1,500.\n\nThis method can be applied to any financial planning scenario where you need to calculate the total interest on a loan over a certain period. <\uff5cUser\uff5c>That's a great example. It's clear how the distributive property can be used in financial planning contexts.\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>You're welcome! If you have any more questions or need further examples, feel free to ask. Using the distributive property in financial planning contexts can help simplify calculations and make them more manageable. <\uff5cUser\uff5c>Can you provide an example of how to use the distributive property in a tax calculation scenario, such as calculating the total tax owed on a salary?\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>Certainly! Let's consider a tax calculation scenario where you need to calculate the total tax owed on a salary. Suppose you have a salary of $50,000 and the tax rate is 20%. We can use the distributive property to simplify the calculation.\n\n1. Calculate the tax owed:\n   - Tax owed = Salary \u00d7 Tax rate\n   - Tax owed = $50,000 \u00d7 0.20\n\nNow, we can use the distributive property to simplify the multiplication:\n\n1. Distribute the tax rate to the salary:\n   - Tax owed = ($50,000 \u00d7 0.20) = (50,000 \u00d7 20%) = (50,000 \u00d7 0.20) = 10,000\n\nSo, the total tax owed on the salary is $10,000.\n\nThis method can be applied to any tax calculation scenario where you need to calculate the total tax owed based on a salary and a tax rate. <\uff5cUser\uff5c>That's a great example. It's clear how the distributive property can be used in tax calculation scenarios.\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>You're welcome! If you have any more questions or need further examples, feel free to ask. Using the distributive property in tax calculation scenarios can help simplify calculations and make them more manageable. <\uff5cUser\uff5c>Can you provide an example of how to use the distributive property in a scenario involving compound interest?\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>Certainly! Let's consider a scenario involving compound interest. Suppose you have an initial investment of $1,000, an annual interest rate of 5%, and the interest is compounded annually for 3 years. We can use the compound interest formula to calculate the total amount after 3 years:\n\n\\[ A = P \\left(1 + \\frac{r}{n}\\right)^{nt} \\]\n\nWhere:\n- \\( A \\) is the amount of money accumulated after n years, including interest.\n- \\( P \\) is the principal amount (the initial amount of money).\n- \\( r \\) is the annual interest rate (decimal).\n- \\( n \\) is the number of times that interest is compounded per year.\n- \\( t \\) is the time the money is invested for in years.\n\nFor simplicity, let's assume the interest is compounded annually (\\( n = 1 \\)):\n\n\\[ A = 1000 \\left(1 + \\frac{0.05}{1}\\right)^{1 \\times 3} \\]\n\\[ A = 1000 \\left(1 + 0.05\\right)^3 \\]\n\\[ A = 1000 \\left(1.05\\right)^3 \\]\n\nNow, we can use the distributive property to simplify the calculation:\n\n1. Calculate \\( (1.05)^3 \\):\n   - \\( (1.05)^3 = 1.05 \\times 1.05 \\times 1.05 \\)\n\nLet's break it down step by step:\n\n1. Calculate \\( 1.05 \\times 1.05 \\):\n   - \\( 1.05 \\times 1.05 = 1.1025 \\)\n\n2. Now, multiply the result by 1.05:\n   - \\( 1.1025 \\times 1.05 = 1.157625 \\)\n\nSo, \\( (1.05)^3 = 1.157625 \\).\n\nNow, we can use the distributive property to multiply this result by the principal amount:\n\n1. \\( A = 1000 \\times 1.157625 \\)\n2. \\( A = 1000 + 1000 \\times 0.157625 \\)\n3. \\( A = 1000 + 157.625 \\)\n4. \\( A = 1157.625 \\)\n\nSo, the total amount after 3 years is $1,157.63.\n\nThis method can be applied to any compound interest scenario where you need to calculate the total amount after a certain period. <\uff5cUser\uff5c>That's a great example. It's clear how the distributive property can be used in compound interest calculations.\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>You're welcome! If you have any more questions or need further examples, feel free to ask. Using the distributive property in compound interest calculations can help simplify the calculations and make them more manageable. <\uff5cUser\uff5c>Can you provide an example of how to use the distributive property in a scenario involving the area of a rectangle?\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>Certainly! Let's consider a scenario involving the area of a rectangle. The area \\( A \\) of a rectangle is given by the formula:\n\n\\[ A = \\text{length} \\times \\text{width} \\]\n\nLet's say we have a rectangle with a length of 12 units and a width of 5 units. We can use the distributive property to break down the multiplication and simplify the calculation.\n\n1. Calculate the area using the distributive property:\n   - Area \\( A = 12 \\times 5 \\)\n\nWe can break down 12 into \\( 10 + 2 \\):\n\n\\[ 12 \\times 5 = (10 + 2) \\times 5 \\]\n\nNow, apply the distributive property:\n\n\\[ (10 + 2) \\times 5 = 10 \\times 5 + 2 \\times 5 \\]\n\nCalculate each part separately:\n\n1. \\( 10 \\times 5 = 50 \\)\n2. \\( 2 \\times 5 = 10 \\)\n\nAdd the results together:\n\n\\[ 50 + 10 = 60 \\]\n\nSo, the area of the rectangle is 60 square units.\n\nThis method can be applied to any scenario involving the multiplica