g the volume of a cone.\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>You're welcome! If you have any more questions or need further examples, feel free to ask. Using the distributive property in calculating the volume of a cone can help simplify the calculations and make them more manageable. <\uff5cUser\uff5c>Can you provide an example of how to use the distributive property in a scenario involving the area of a rhombus?\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>Certainly! Let's consider a scenario involving the area of a rhombus. The area \\( A \\) of a rhombus is given by the formula:\n\n\\[ A = \\text{side} \\times \\text{height} \\]\n\nHowever, if we want to use the distributive property, we can use the alternative formula that involves the diagonals of the rhombus:\n\n\\[ A = \\frac{1}{2} \\times d_1 \\times d_2 \\]\n\nWhere:\n- \\( d_1 \\) and \\( d_2 \\) are the lengths of the diagonals.\n\nLet's say we have a rhombus with diagonals \\( d_1 = 8 \\) units and \\( d_2 = 6 \\) units. We can use the distributive property to break down the multiplication and simplify the calculation.\n\n1. Calculate the area using the distributive property:\n   - Area \\( A = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 8 \\times 6 \\)\n\nWe can break down 8 into \\( 4 \\times 2 \\):\n\n\\[ \\frac{1}{2} \\times 8 \\times 6 = \\frac{1}{2} \\times (4 \\times 2) \\times 6 \\]\n\nNow, apply the distributive property:\n\n\\[ \\frac{1}{2} \\times (4 \\times 2) \\times 6 = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 4 \\times (2 \\times 6) \\]\n\nCalculate each part separately:\n\n1. \\( 2 \\times 6 = 12 \\)\n2. \\( \\frac{1}{2} \\times 4 = 2 \\)\n3. \\( 2 \\times 12 = 24 \\)\n\nSo, the area of the rhombus is 24 square units.\n\nThis method can be applied to any scenario involving the multiplication of a fraction with a product, making the calculation more manageable. <\uff5cUser\uff5c>That's a great example. It's clear how the distributive property can be used in calculating the area of a rhombus.\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>You're welcome! If you have any more questions or