:\n- \\( h \\) is the height of the frustum.\n- \\( R_1 \\) is the radius of the larger base.\n- \\( R_2 \\) is the radius of the smaller base.\n\nLet's say we have a frustum of a cone with:\n- Height \\( h = 6 \\) units,\n- Radius of the larger base \\( R_1 = 5 \\) units,\n- Radius of the smaller base \\( R_2 = 3 \\) units.\n\nWe can use the volume formula:\n\n\\[ V = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi \\times 6 \\times (5^2 + 5 \\times 3 + 3^2) \\]\n\nFirst, calculate the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n\\[ 5^2 + 5 \\times 3 + 3^2 = 25 + 15 + 9 = 49 \\]\n\nNow, substitute this back into the volume formula:\n\n\\[ V = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi \\times 6 \\times 49 \\]\n\nWe can use the distributive property to simplify the multiplication:\n\n\\[ V = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi \\times (6 \\times 49) \\]\n\nNow, apply the distributive property:\n\n\\[ \\frac{1}{3} \\pi \\times (6 \\times 49) = \\frac{1}{3} \\pi \\times 294 \\]\n\nCalculate the final multiplication:\n\n\\[ \\frac{1}{3} \\pi \\times 294 = 98\\pi \\]\n\nSo, the volume of the frustum of the cone is \\( 98\\pi \\) cubic units.\n\nThis method can be applied to any scenario involving the multiplication of a constant with a product, making the calculation more manageable. <\uff5cUser\uff5c>That's a great example. It's clear how the distributive property can be used in calculating the volume of a frustum of a cone.\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>You're welcome! If you have any more questions or need further examples, feel free to ask. Using the distributive property in calculating the volume of a frustum of a cone can help simplify the calculations and make them more manageable. <\uff5cUser\uff5c>Can you provide an example of how to use the distributive property in a scenario involving the area of a parallelogram with a trapezoid base?\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>Certainly! Let's consider a scenario involving the area of a parallelogram with a trapezoid base. The area of a parallelogram is given by the formula:\n\n\\[ A = \\text{base} \\times \\text{height} \\]\n\nLet's say we have a parallelogram with a base that is a trapezoid. The trapezoid has bases \\( b_1 = 8 \\) units and \\( b_2 = 6 \\) units, and a height of 4 units. The area of the trapezoid is:\n\n\\[ \\text{Area of trapezoid} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times (b_1 + b_2) \\times \\text{height} \\]\n\\[ \\text{Area of trapezoid} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times (8 + 6) \\times 4 \\]\n\\[ \\text{Area of trapezoid} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 14 \\times 4 \\]\n\\[ \\text{Area of trapezoid} = 7 \\times 4 \\]\n\\[ \\text{Area of trapezoid} = 28 \\text{ square units} \\]\n\nNow, we can use this area as the base of the parallelogram. The height of the parallelogram is the same as the height of the trapezoid, which is 4 units. So, the area of the parallelogram is:\n\n\\[ A = \\text{base} \\times \\text{height} \\]\n\\[ A = 28 \\times 4 \\]\n\nWe can use the distributive property to simplify the multiplication:\n\n\\[ A = 28 \\times 4 = (20 + 8) \\times 4 \\]\n\\[ A = 20 \\times 4 + 8 \\times 4 \\]\n\\[ A = 80 + 32 \\]\n\\[ A = 112 \\text{ square units} \\]\n\nSo, the area of the parallelogram is 112 square units.\n\nThis method can be applied to any scenario involving the multiplication of a fraction with a sum, making the calculation more manageable. <\uff5cUser\uff5c>That's a great example. It's clear how the distributive property can be used in calculating the area of a parallelogram with a trapezoid base.\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>You're welcome! If you have any more questions or need further examples, feel free to ask. Using the distributive property in calculating the area of a parallelogram with a trapezoid base can help simplify the calculations and make them more manageable. <\uff5cUser\uff5c>Can you provide an example of how to use the distributive property in a scenario involving the volume of a sphere with a cylindrical hole?\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>Certainly! Let's consider a scenario involving the volume of a sphere with a cylindrical hole. The volume of a sphere is given by the formula:\n\n\\[ V_{\\text{sphere}} = \\frac{4}{3} \\pi r^3 \\]\n\nWhere \\( r \\) is the radius of the sphere.\n\nThe volume of a cylinder is given by the formula:\n\n\\[ V_{\\text{cylinder}} = \\pi r^2 h \\]\n\nWhere \\( r \\) is the radius of the cylinder and \\( h \\) is the height of the cylinder.\n\nLet's say we have a sphere with a radius of 3 units and a cylindrical hole with a radius of 1 unit and a height of 6 units. We need to find the volume of the remaining solid after the cylindrical hole is removed.\n\n1. Calculate the volume of the sphere:\n   \\[ V_{\\text{sphere}} = \\frac{4}{3} \\pi (3)^3 \\]\n   \\[ V_{\\text{sphere}} = \\frac{4}{3} \\pi \\times 27 \\]\n   \\[ V_{\\text{sphere}} = 36\\pi \\]\n\n2. Calculate the volume of the cylindrical hole:\n   \\[ V_{\\text{cylinder}} = \\pi (1)^2 \\times 6 \\]\n   \\[ V_{\\text{cylinder}} = \\pi \\times 6 \\]\n   \\[ V_{\\text{cylinder}} = 6\\pi \\]\n\n3. Subtract the volume of the cylindrical hole from the volume of the sphere to find the volume of the remaining solid:\n   \\[ V_{\\text{remaining}} = V_{\\text{sphere}} - V_{\\text{cylinder}} \\]\n   \\[ V_{\\text{remaining}} = 36\\pi - 6\\pi \\]\n   \\[ V_{\\text{remaining}} = 30\\pi \\]\n\nThis method can be applied to any scenario involving the subtraction of volumes, making the calculation more manageable. <\uff5cUser\uff5c>That's a great example. It's clear how the distributive property can be used in calculating the volume of a sphere with a cylindrical hole.\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>You're welcome! If you have any more questions or need further examples, feel free to ask. Using the distributive property in calculating the volume of a sphere with a cylindrical hole can help simplify the calculations and make them more manageable. <\uff5cUser\uff5c>Can you provide an example of how to use the distributive property in a scenario involving the area of a rectangle with a semicircle on one side?\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>Certainly! Let's consider a s