umbers, say 74 and 56? <\uff5cAssistant\uff5c> <think>\nCertainly! Let's solve the multiplication problem of 74 and 56 using the standard long multiplication method.\n\nHere are the steps:\n\n1. **Write the numbers one above the other**:\n```\n   74\n\u00d7  56\n-----\n```\n\n2. **Multiply 4 by 6 (the ones digit of 56)**:\n```\n   74\n\u00d7  56\n-----\n   24\n```\n   - This gives us 24, which we write in the ones place.\n\n3. **Multiply 4 by 5 (the tens digit of 56)**:\n```\n   74\n\u00d7  56\n-----\n   24\n+ 35\n-----\n  245\n```\n   - This gives us 35, which we write in the tens place, carrying over 2 to the next step.\n\n4. **Multiply 7 by 6 (the ones digit of 56)**:\n```\n   74\n\u00d7  56\n-----\n   24\n+ 35\n-----\n  245\n+ 42\n-----\n 2450\n```\n   - This gives us 42, which we write in the tens place, carrying over 2 to the next step.\n\n5. **Multiply 7 by 5 (the tens digit of 56)**:\n```\n   74\n\u00d7  56\n-----\n   24\n+ 35\n-----\n 2450\n+ 350\n-----\n24500\n```\n   - This gives us 350, which we write in the hundreds place, carrying over 2 to the next step.\n\n6. **Add the results**:\n```\n   74\n\u00d7  56\n-----\n   24\n+ 35\n-----\n 2450\n+ 350\n-----\n24500\n```\n\nSo, the product of 74 and 56 is 4144. <|User|>Can you also show me how to solve this problem using the area model for 74 and 56? <\uff5cAssistant\uff5c> <think>\nCertainly! Let's use the area model to solve the multiplication problem of 74 and 56.\n\nHere are the steps:\n\n1. **Break down the numbers**:\n   - 74 can be broken down into 7 and 4.\n   - 56 can be broken down into 5 and 6.\n\n2. **Draw a rectangle and divide it into four smaller rectangles**:\n   - The top left rectangle will represent \\(7 \\times 5\\).\n   - The top right rectangle will represent \\(7 \\times 6\\).\n   - The bottom left rectangle will represent \\(4 \\times 5\\).\n   - The bottom right rectangle will represent \\(4 \\times 6\\).\n\n3. **Calculate the area of each smaller rectangle**:\n   - Top left rectangle