he steps:\n\n1. **Write the numbers one above the other**:\n```\n   82\n\u00d7  65\n-----\n```\n\n2. **Multiply 2 by 5 (the ones digit of 65)**:\n```\n   82\n\u00d7  65\n-----\n   10\n```\n   - This gives us 10, which we write in the ones place.\n\n3. **Multiply 2 by 6 (the tens digit of 65)**:\n```\n   82\n\u00d7  65\n-----\n   10\n+ 48\n-----\n  140\n```\n   - This gives us 48, which we write in the tens place, carrying over 1 to the next step.\n\n4. **Multiply 8 by 5 (the ones digit of 65)**:\n```\n   82\n\u00d7  65\n-----\n   10\n+ 48\n-----\n  140\n+ 40\n-----\n 1400\n```\n   - This gives us 40, which we write in the tens place, carrying over 1 to the next step.\n\n5. **Multiply 8 by 6 (the tens digit of 65)**:\n```\n   82\n\u00d7  65\n-----\n   10\n+ 48\n-----\n 1400\n+ 480\n-----\n14000\n```\n   - This gives us 480, which we write in the hundreds place, carrying over 1 to the next step.\n\n6. **Add the results**:\n```\n   82\n\u00d7  65\n-----\n   10\n+ 48\n-----\n 1400\n+ 480\n-----\n14000\n```\n\nSo, the product of 82 and 65 is 5330. <|User|>Can you also show me how to solve this problem using the area model for 82 and 65? <\uff5cAssistant\uff5c> <think>\nCertainly! Let's use the area model to solve the multiplication problem of 82 and 65.\n\nHere are the steps:\n\n1. **Break down the numbers**:\n   - 82 can be broken down into 8 and 2.\n   - 65 can be broken down into 6 and 5.\n\n2. **Draw a rectangle and divide it into four smaller rectangles**:\n   - The top left rectangle will represent \\(8 \\times 6\\).\n   - The top right rectangle will represent \\(8 \\times 5\\).\n   - The bottom left rectangle will represent \\(2 \\times 6\\).\n   - The bottom right rectangle will represent \\(2 \\times 5\\).\n\n3. **Calculate the area of each smaller rectangle**:\n   - Top left rectangle: \\(8 \\times 6 = 48\\)\n   - Top right rectangle: \\(8 \\times 5 = 40\\)\n   - Bottom left rectangle: \\(2 \\times 6 = 12\\)\n   - Bottom right rectangle: \\(2 \\times 5 = 10\\)