      "text": "02535. <|User|>I understand the lattice method for this example. Can you show me how to solve \\(09 \\times 46\\) using the standard long multiplication method? <\uff5cAssistant\uff5c> <think>\nCertainly! Let's solve \\(09 \\times 46\\) using the standard long multiplication method. Here are the steps:\n\n1. **Write the numbers in a vertical format:**\n   ```\n   09\n\u00d7 46\n```\n\n2. **Multiply 9 by 6 (the units digit of 46):**\n   ```\n   09\n\u00d7 46\n-----\n  54\n   ```\n\n3. **Multiply 9 by 4 (the tens digit of 46), and remember to shift one place to the left:**\n   ```\n   09\n\u00d7 46\n-----\n  54\n+360\n   ```\n\n4. **Add the results:**\n   ```\n   09\n\u00d7 46\n-----\n  54\n+360\n-----\n 414\n   ```\n\nSo, the product of \\(09 \\times 46\\) is \\(414\\). <|User|>Can you show me how to solve \\(09 \\times 46\\) using the partial products method? <\uff5cAssistant\uff5c> <think>\nCertainly! The partial products method involves breaking down each number into its place values and then multiplying each part separately before summing the results. Let's solve \\(09 \\times 46\\) using the partial products method:\n\n1. **Break down the numbers into their place values:**\n   - \\(09\\) can be broken down into \\(0 \\times 10 + 9 \\times 1 = 0 + 9 = 9\\)\n   - \\(46\\) can be broken down into \\(4 \\times 10 + 6 \\times 1 = 40 + 6 = 46\\)\n\n2. **Multiply each part of the first number by each part of the second number:**\n   - \\(9 \\times 40 = 360\\)\n   - \\(9 \\times 6 = 54\\)\n\n3. **Add the partial products together:**\n   - \\(360 + 54 = 414\\)\n\nSo, the product of \\(09 \\times 46\\) is \\(414\\). <|User|>Can you show me how to solve \\(09 \\times 46\\) using the grid method? <\uff5cAssistant\uff5c> <think>\nCertainly! The grid method, also known as the box method, involves breaking down each number into its place values and then multiplying each part of one number by each part of the other number, before summing the results. Let's solve \\(09 \\times 46\\) using the grid method:\n\n1. **Break down the numbers into their place values:**\n   - \\(09\\) can be broken down into \\(0 \\times 10 + 9 \\times 1 = 0 + 9 = 9\\)\n   - \\(46\\) can be broken down into \\(4 \\times 10 + 6 \\times 1 = 40 + 6 = 46\\)\n\n2. **Set up the grid:**\n   ```\n   9 | 0\n   ---------\n   40 | 6\n   ```\n\n3. **Multiply each part of the first number by each part of the second number:**\n   - \\(9 \\times 40 = 360\\)\n   - \\(9 \\times 6 = 54\\)\n\n4. **Add the partial products together:**\n   - \\(360 + 54 = 414\\)\n\nSo, the product of \\(09 \\times 46\\) is \\(414\\). <|User|>Can you show me how to solve \\(09 \\times 46\\) using the area model? <\uff5cAssistant\uff5c> <think>\nCertainly! The area model for multiplication involves breaking down each number into its place values and then visualizing the multiplication as the area of a rectangle. Let's solve \\(09 \\times 46\\) using the area model:\n\n1. **Break down the numbers into their place values:**\n   - \\(09\\) can be broken down into \\(0 \\times 10 + 9 \\times 1 = 0 + 9 = 9\\)\n   - \\(46\\) can be broken down into \\(4 \\times 10 + 6 \\times 1 = 40 + 6 = 46\\)\n\n2. **Set up the area model:**\n   - The area model will be a rectangle divided into four smaller rectangles.\n   - The dimensions of the large rectangle will be \\(9\\) (from \\(09\\)) and \\(46\\) (from \\(46\\)).\n\n3. **Calculate the area of each smaller rectangle:**\n   - The area of the top-left rectangle is \\(9 \\times 40 = 360\\)\n   - The area of the top-right rectangle is \\(9 \\times 6 = 54\\)\n   - The area of the bottom-left rectangle is \\(0 \\times 40 = 0\\)\n   - The area of the bottom-right rectangle is \\(0 \\times 6 = 0\\)\n\n4. **Sum the areas of the smaller rectangles:**\n   - \\(360 + 54 + 0 + 0 = 414\\)\n\nSo, the product of \\(09 \\times 46\\) is \\(414\\). <|User|>Can you show me how to solve \\(09 \\times 46\\) using the standard long multiplication method again? <\uff5cAssis