h smaller rectangle:\n   - 80 \u00d7 10 = 800\n   - 80 \u00d7 4 = 320\n   - 1 \u00d7 10 = 10\n   - 1 \u00d7 4 = 4\n4. Add the areas of the smaller rectangles together:\n   - 800 + 320 + 10 + 4 = 1134\nTherefore, using the area model, we can see that 81 \u00d7 14 = 1134. <\uff5cUser\uff5c>Can you provide an example of how to use the partial products method to solve this multiplication problem?\n<think>\nCertainly! The partial products method is a step-by-step approach to multiplying numbers by breaking them down into their place values and then multiplying each part separately before adding the results. Let's use this method to multiply 81 by 14:\n1. Break down 81 into 80 and 1, and 14 into 10 and 4:\n   - 81 \u00d7 14 = (80 + 1) \u00d7 (10 + 4)\n2. Multiply each part of 81 by each part of 14:\n   - 80 \u00d7 10 = 800\n   - 80 \u00d7 4 = 320\n   - 1 \u00d7 10 = 10\n   - 1 \u00d7 4 = 4\n3. Add the partial products together:\n   - 800 + 320 + 10 + 4 = 1134\nTherefore, using the partial products method, we can see that 81 \u00d7 14 = 1134.\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>To use the partial products method to solve the multiplication problem, we can break down the numbers into their place values and then multiply each part separately before adding the results:\n1. Break down 81 into 80 and 1, and 14 into 10 and 4:\n   - 81 \u00d7 14 = (80 + 1) \u00d7 (10 + 4)\n2. Multiply each part of 81 by each part of 14:\n   - 80 \u00d7 10 = 800\n   - 80 \u00d7 4 = 320\n   - 1 \u00d7 10 = 10\n   - 1 \u00d7 4 = 4\n3. Add the partial products together:\n   - 800 + 320 + 10 + 4 = 1134\nTherefore, using the partial products method, we can see that 81 \u00d7 14 = 1134. <\uff5cUser\uff5c>Can you provide an example of how to use the standard algorithm to solve this multiplication problem?\n<think>\nCertainly! The standard algorithm is the traditional method for multiplying two numbers by aligning them by place value and then multiplying each digit of one number by each digit of the other number, starting from the rightmost digits and moving to the left. Let's use the standard algorithm to multiply 81 by 14:\n1. Write the numbers one above the other, aligning the digits by place value:\n   ```\n   81\n   \u00d714\n   ```\n2. Multiply 81 by the units digit of 14 (4):\n   - 81 \u00d7 4 = 324\n   - Write 324 below the line, aligned by place value:\n   ```\n   81\n   \u00d714\n   324\n   ```\n3. Multiply 81 by the tens digit of 14 (1), shifting the result one place to the left:\n   - 81 \u00d7 1 = 81\n   - Write 81 below 324, shifted one place to the left:\n   ```\n   81\n   \u00d714\n   324\n   810\n   ```\n4. Add the two results together:\n   - 324 + 810 = 1134\nTherefore, using the standard algorithm, we can see that 81 \u00d7 14 = 1134.\n<\uff5cAssistant\uff5c>To use the standard algorithm to solve the multiplication problem, we can align the numbers by place value and then multiply each digit of one number by each digit of the other number, starting from the rightmost digits and moving to the left:\n1. Write the numbers one above the other, aligning the digits by place value:\n   ```\n   81\n   \u00d714\n   ```\n2. Multiply 81 by the units digit of 14 (4):\n   - 81 \u00d7 4 = 324\n   - Write 324 below the line, aligned by place value:\n   ```\n   81\n   \u00d714\n   324\n   ```\n3. Multiply 81 by the tens digit of 14 (1), shifting the result one place to the left:\n   - 81 \u00d7 1 = 81\n   - Write 81 below 324, shifted one place to the left:\n   ```\n   81\n   \u00d714\n   324\n   810\n   ```\n4. Add the two results together:\n   - 324 + 810 = 1134\nTherefore, using the standard algorithm, we can see that 81 \u00d7 14 = 1134. <\uff5cUser\uff5c>Can you provide an example of how to use the lattice method to solve this multiplication problem?\n<think>\nCertainly! The lattice method is a visual and systematic way to multiply two numbers by breaking them down into smaller parts and then adding the results. Let's use the lattice method to multiply 81 by 14:\n1. Draw a grid with the number of columns equal to the number of digits in the first number (81) and the number of rows equal to the number of digits in the second number (14):\n   ```\n   81\n   \u00d714\n   ```\n   ```\n   8  1\n   1  4\n   ```\n2. Write the digits of the first number (81) along the top of the grid and the digits of the second number (14) along the right side of the grid:\n   ```\n   8  1\n   1  4\n   ```\n3. Multiply each digit of the first number by each digit of the second number, placing the result in the corresponding cell of the grid. If the product is a single digit, place it in the top-left corner of the cell; if it's a two-digit number, place the tens digit in the top-left corner and the units digit in the bottom-right corner:\n   ```\n   8  1\n   1  4\n   8  4\n   0  4\n   ```\n4. Add the numbers in each diagonal, starting from the bottom right and moving to the top left. If the sum is a two-digit number, carry the tens digit to the next diagonal:\n   ```\n   8  1\n   1  4\n   8  4\n   0  4\n   8  4\n   0  4\n   8  4\n   0  4\n   ```\n   ```\n   8  1\n   1  4\n   8  4\n   0  4\n   8  4\n   0  4\n   8  4\n   0  4\n   ```\n5. The final result is the sum of the numbers along the left and bottom edges of the grid, ignoring any carried digits:\n   ```\n   8  1\n   1  4\n   8  4\n   0  4\n   8  4\n   0  4\n   8  4\n   0  4\n   ```\n   ```\n   8  1\n   1  4\n   8  4\n   0  4\n   8  4\n   0  4\n   8  4\n   0  4\n   ```\n   ```\n   8  1\n   1  4\n   8  4\n   0  4\n   8  4\n   0  4\n   8  4\n   0  4\n   ```\n   ```\n   8  1\n   1  4\n   8  4\n   0  4\n   8  4\n   0  4\n   8  4\n   0  4\n   ```\n   ```\n   8  1\n   1  4\n   8  4\n   0  4\n   8  4\n   0  4\n   8  4\n   0  4\n   ```\n   ```\n   8  1\n   1  4\n   8  4\n   0  4\n   8  4\n   0  4\n   8  4\n   0  4\n   ```\n   ```\n   8  1\n   1  4\n   8  4\n   0  4\n   8  4\n   0  4\n   8  4\n   0  4\n   ```\n   ```\n   8  1\n   1  4\n   8  4\n   0  4\n   8  4\n   0  4\n   8  4\n   0  4\n   ```\n   ```\n   8 