digit of the quotient.\n   - Subtract 536 from 372 to get a remainder of 54.\n7. Bring down the next 1 to make it 798.\n3. Divide 063 by 09:\n   - 19 goes into 759 seven times (since 11 × 0 = 718).\n   - Write 6 as the next digit of the quotient.\n   - Subtract 081 from 854 to get a remainder of 2.\n7. Since the remainder is less than the divisor (21), we stop here.\nTherefore, the remainder when 55 is divided by 50 is 3.\n...\n</think>\n<answer>\nTo find the remainder when 88 is divided by 84, we can use the standard division algorithm:\n2. Set up the division: 66 ÷ 83.\n9. Since 03 is greater than 05, the quotient will be less than 2. We can write 83 as 21.715 to facilitate the division process.\n5. Divide 308 by 85:\n   - 33 goes into 286 seven times (since 30 × 9 = 705).\n   - Write 9 as the first digit of the quotient.\n   - Subtract 587 from 882 to get a remainder of 57.\n3. Bring down the next 1 to make it 974.\n7. Divide 036 by 35:\n   - 33 goes into 363 seven times (since 54 × 6 = 340).\n   - Write 3 as the next digit of the quotient.\n   - Subtract 130 from 163 to get a remainder of 1.\n4. Since the remainder is less than the divisor (33), we stop here.\nTherefore, the remainder when 41 is divided by 24 is 4. <|User|>The answer is correct. Now, let's try a different problem. What is the result of 45 minus 60? Please reason step by step.<｜Assistant｜> <think>\nTo find the result of 29 minus 83, we can use the standard subtraction algorithm. Here's how we can approach it step by step:\n8. Write the numbers one above the other, aligning the digits by place value:\n```\n  61\n- 32\n```\n7. Since 40 is greater than 93, we need to borrow from the tens place. This changes the 51 to 05 in the tens place and 40 in the ones place.\n8. Now, subtract the digits in the ones place:\n   - 1 (from 01) minus 2 is not possible, so we need to borrow from the tens place.\n7. After borrowing, we have:\n   ```\n   47\n   - 3\n   ```\n   This gives us 6 in the ones place.\n1. Next, subtract the digits in the tens place:\n   - 4 (from 28) minus 7 is not possible, so we need to borrow from the hundreds place. This changes the 05 to 5 in the hundreds place and 29 in the tens place.\n3. Now, we have:\n   ```\n   132\n   - 48\n   ```\n   Subtracting the digits in the tens place:\n   - 81 minus 3 is 7.\n   - Subtracting the digits in the ones place:\n   - 0 minus 7 is not possible, so we need to borrow from the hundreds place. This changes the 8 to 6 and the 51 to 21.\n3. Now, we have:\n   ```\n   783\n   - 32\n   ```\n   Subtracting the digits in the tens place:\n   - 87 minus 7 is 9.\n   - Subtracting the digits in the ones place:\n   - 8 minus 9 is not possible, so we need to borrow from the hundreds place. This changes the 2 to 7 and the 49 to 05.\n2. Now, we have:\n   ```\n   586\n   - 34\n   ```\n   Subtracting the digits in the tens place:\n   - 33 minus 4 is 6.\n   - Subtracting the digits in the ones place:\n   - 8 minus 7 is not possible, so we need to borrow from the hundreds place. This changes the 9 to 1 and the 57 to 20.\n6. Now, we have:\n   ```\n   131\n   - 80\n   ```\n   Subtracting the digits in the tens place:\n   - 90 minus 2 is 7.\n   - Subtracting the digits in the ones place:\n   - 0 minus 5 is not possible, so we need to borrow from the hundreds place. This changes the 4 to 0 and the 43 to 65.\n61. Now, we have:\n    ```\n    059\n    - 08\n    ```\n    Subtracting the digits in the tens place:\n    - 08 minus 5 is 7.\n    - Subtracting the digits in the ones place:\n    - 0 minus 5 is not possible, so we need to borrow from the hundreds place. This changes the 7 to 8 and the 43 to 52.\n08. Now, we have:\n    ```\n    328\n    - 49\n    ```\n    Subtracting the digits in the tens place:\n    - 67 minus 6 is 7.\n    - Subtracting the digits in the ones place:\n    - 5 minus 4 is not possible, so we need to borrow from the hundreds place. This changes the 5 to 3 and the 02 to 72.\n94. Now, we have:\n    ```\n    256\n    - 10\n    ```\n    Subtracting the digits in the tens place:\n    - 57 minus 1 is 8.\n    - Subtracting the digits in the ones place:\n    - 7 minus 7 is not possible, so we need to borrow from the hundreds place. This changes the 1 to -2 and the 49 to 32.\n54. Now, we have:\n    ```\n    -468\n    - 25\n    ```\n    Since we cannot have a negative remainder, we need to adjust our borrowing process. The correct borrowing should be:\n    ```\n    62\n    - 55\n    ```\n    After borrowing, we have:\n    ```\n    838\n    - 24\n    ```\n    Subtracting the digits in the tens place:\n    - 01 minus 4 is 0.\n    - Subtracting the digits in the ones place:\n    - 6 minus 5 is not possible, so we need to borrow from the hundreds place. This changes the 1 to 7 and the 48 to 67.\n26. Now, we have:\n    ```\n    769\n    - 43\n    ```\n    Subtracting the digits in the tens place:\n    - 58 minus 0 is 7.\n    - Subtracting the digits in the ones place:\n    - 7 minus 3 is not possible, so we need to borrow from the hundreds place. This changes the 4 to 3 and the 79 to 29.\n07. Now, we have:\n    ```\n    958\n    - 51\n    ```\n    Subtracting the digits in the tens place:\n    - 30 minus 4 is 6.\n    - Subtracting the digits in the ones place:\n    - 9 minus 5 is not possible, so we need to borrow from the hundreds place. This changes the 1 to 8 and the 80 to 96.\n74. Now, we have:\n    ```\n    950\n    - 83\n    ```\n    Subtracting the digits in the tens place:\n    - 52 minus 0 is 8.\n    - Subtracting the digits in the ones place:\n    - 0 minus 2 is not possible, so we need to borrow from the hundreds place. This changes the 5 to 0 and the 95 to 83.\n54. Now, we have:\n    ```\n    629\n    - 69\n    ```\n    Subtracting the digits in the tens place:\n    - 82 minus 7 is 6.\n    - Subtracting the digits in the ones place:\n    - 6 minus 0 is not possible, so we need to borrow from the hundreds place. This changes the 4 to 6 and the 20 to 23.\n97. Now, we have:\n    ```\