n   \u00d7 31\n   ```\n\n2. Multiply 7 by 1 (the units digit of 31):\n   ```\n     67\n   \u00d7 31\n   -----\n      7\n   ```\n\n3. Multiply 7 by 30 (the tens digit of 31, which is 3 multiplied by 10):\n   ```\n     67\n   \u00d7 31\n   -----\n      7\n   210\n   ```\n\n4. Multiply 60 by 1 (the units digit of 31):\n   ```\n     67\n   \u00d7 31\n   -----\n      7\n   210\n   66\n   ```\n\n5. Multiply 60 by 30 (the tens digit of 31, which is 3 multiplied by 10):\n   ```\n     67\n   \u00d7 31\n   -----\n      7\n   210\n   66\n   1800\n   ```\n\n6. Add the results of the partial products:\n   ```\n     67\n   \u00d7 31\n   -----\n      7\n   210\n   66\n   1800\n   -----\n   2077\n   ```\n\nTherefore, using the standard algorithm, we get the product of 67 and 31 as 2077. <\uff5cUser\uff5c>Could you also explain how to solve this using the partial products method?\n<think>\nCertainly! The partial products method involves breaking down each number into its place values and then multiplying each part separately before adding the results. For the multiplication of 67 and 31, we can break down each number as follows:\n- 67 can be broken down into 60 and 7\n- 31 can be broken down into 30 and 1\n\nWe then multiply each part of the first number by each part of the second number and add the results. Here's the step-by-step process:\n\n1. Multiply 60 by 30:\n   60 \u00d7 30 = 1800\n\n2. Multiply 60 by 1:\n   60 \u00d7 1 = 60\n\n3. Multiply 7 by 30:\n   7 \u00d7 30 = 210\n\n4. Multiply 7 by 1:\n   7 \u00d7 1 = 7\n\nNow, we add all these partial products together:\n1800 + 60 + 210 + 7 = 2077\n\nTherefore, using the partial products method, we get the product of 67 and 31 as 2077.\n...\n</think>\n<answer>\nCertainly! Using the partial products method, we break down each number into its place values and then multiply each part separately before adding the results. Here's how we can solve 67 \u00d7 31 using this method:\n\n1. Break down 67 into 60 and 7, and 31 into 30 and 1.\n2.